WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.760
A Shepard Smith gave a very interesting interview&nbsp;
to Christiane Amanpour the other day. Shepard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:12.560
Smith, former Fox News anchor for a long time,&nbsp;
was like the sole voice of reason on Fox News.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.160
And when he was there, we wondered, does CHEP&nbsp;
understand what he's doing? Does he understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:26.080
context of crazy in which he is offering something&nbsp;
that looks more like the news on Fox News Channel?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:31.120
And this interview he gave to Christiane Amanpour&nbsp;
is fascinating. And it confirms that Shep Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:38.160
was very much aware of what he was doing&nbsp;
there. And he said that a lot of his presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.000
on Fox News was specifically to serve as a&nbsp;
counterpoint to the wackiness of what we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.920
seeing on Fox News. He called it injurious&nbsp;
to society, what was happening there. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.760
you know, this plays into our discussion about&nbsp;
the future of right wing media in the Trump era.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:02.800
Smith says in this interview that the falsehoods&nbsp;
from his colleagues on Fox News eventually became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.680
too much to bear, but that his point was&nbsp;
to inject reality. Take a look at this.&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.680
I thought it was important that I stay there&nbsp;
because I knew that I was grounded in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:18.240
philosophy and and working toward that promise.&nbsp;
And if you feel like the Fox viewers were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.640
Getting myths or disinformation, I was there to&nbsp;
make sure that they got it straight, there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.680
a lot of others in there who were trying to do&nbsp;
the same thing. But I thought to just abandon it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.800
and to deprive those viewers of it wasn't just me.&nbsp;
I mean, there's an entire team of people getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.080
the news to the air to to deny them that with the&nbsp;
thought that they might replace it with opinion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.240
instead seemed a little selfish.
This is actually very interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.720
staying at Fox News, because if you leave,&nbsp;
they replace you with someone worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:57.200
We applied that same thought to the Trump&nbsp;
administration in many cases. We said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.880
you know, Foushee clearly is disgusted with what's&nbsp;
going on, but if he quits and they replace him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:09.840
with someone, even worse, is the country better&nbsp;
off or worse off? And that's the exact analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.280
that QEP says he was doing when at FOX News.
Christiane Amanpour then asks whether Shep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.240
Smith accepts that Fox perpetuates&nbsp;
division and perpetuates lies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.760
And Shep gives a very interesting answer.&nbsp;
I want to play the entire thing for you.&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.160
And I don't know, you know, you&nbsp;
can be proud of what you did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.760
but I wonder whether you also feel now that CNBC,&nbsp;
you see how a whole different ecosystem works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:41.600
whether you accept that Fox News in the opinion&nbsp;
sphere and, you know, in other Rupert Murdoch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:48.560
enterprises, you know, perpetuated so many&nbsp;
of the divisions, the lies, the conspiracies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.080
the support. Look at Sean&nbsp;
Hannity going up and, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:58.960
doing campaign rallies for President Trump. So&nbsp;
blurring the lines is to be the propaganda arm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.840
of the Republican Party. I just&nbsp;
wonder what you think about that now.&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.600
Well, I feel the same way&nbsp;
about that now that I did then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:16.320
and my goal was to just keep the blinders&nbsp;
on and do my job to the best of my ability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:24.000
And was I I am always concerned when&nbsp;
people want to opine all you like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.720
But if you're going to opine, begin with the truth&nbsp;
and opine from there. And it's that that deviation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.920
from that that has has caused me the greatest&nbsp;
concern. And I and I believe that when people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.880
begin with a false premise and lead people&nbsp;
astray, that's injurious to society. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.600
and it's the antithesis of what we should&nbsp;
be doing. Those of us who are so honored and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:52.000
and grateful to have a platform of influence, who&nbsp;
have to use it for for the public good. And and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.480
slept very well. I don't know how some people&nbsp;
sleep at night because I know that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:02.320
a lot of people who who have propagated the lies&nbsp;
and have pushed them forward over and over again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:09.120
who are smart enough and educated enough to know&nbsp;
better. And I hope that at some point, those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:15.040
have done us harm as a nation, and I might even&nbsp;
add as a world, will look around and realize what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.080
they've done, but I'm not holding my breath.
So that's a really interesting interview.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.480
And from the outside, we didn't know what&nbsp;
Szeps perception was of what he was doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:32.000
And some say Shep was still bad because he&nbsp;
was on a network generating them profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.040
which were then funneled into continuing the&nbsp;
disinformation. And I sympathize with that. Shep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.720
was a cog in the wheel, and you could even make&nbsp;
the case that Shep provided cover to Fox News.&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:50.320
You could say, Shep, being there one hour a&nbsp;
day, five days a week, provided cover to Fox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:56.320
to say, no, no, no. We have all sorts of&nbsp;
different perspectives. But the reality is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:03.280
you know, Fox would exist either way. And if you&nbsp;
want to say, are we better off with Fox having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.720
one hour of Shep Smith versus zero&nbsp;
or replacing Shep with someone worse?&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.240
Yeah, you know, I mean, Shep knew what Fox&nbsp;
was every time he was cashing the checks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:23.280
And I don't know what that means. If Shep's goal&nbsp;
was to provide a counter narrative, which he did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.960
he could have left earlier and gotten&nbsp;
the same paycheck elsewhere. And in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:34.000
now he's getting the paycheck at CNBC. It's not&nbsp;
that clear to me that Shep did something wrong by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:39.040
staying when he was providing a counterpoint. Now,&nbsp;
another perspective is he should be credited for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.880
remaining true to his role, despite so many people&nbsp;
around him at Fox News selling themselves out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:50.320
and that his responsibility was to stay as&nbsp;
long as possible and for for as long as Fox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.800
would allow him to stay and not fire him, which&nbsp;
they ultimately didn't. Ultimately, he quit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.480
although maybe he was pressured. We&nbsp;
don't know he's doing a good thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:04.240
And I can sympathize with different&nbsp;
perspectives on this. But I think much like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.640
with the Foushee example, if Foushee quit&nbsp;
when Trump talked about injecting bleach and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.560
started attacking Foushee publicly, if Foushee&nbsp;
quits, then he's replaced with someone that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.680
probably going to do far more damage and probably&nbsp;
lead to even more deaths than we've already seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.720
during the coronavirus pandemic. So Falchi stayed&nbsp;
and it was probably the right thing to do much the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:32.160
same way. I think on balance, we are better off&nbsp;
that Shepp stayed longer rather than leaving and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.680
being replaced with, you know, imagine if Shepp&nbsp;
leaves a year and a half ago or whatever it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.880
and they replaced him with Maria Bartiromo. The&nbsp;
country's worse off, there's no question about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:48.320
So I'm curious to hear your thoughts on that.
You can find me on Twitter at David Pakman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.920
And we have a fantastic Friday&nbsp;
show planned for you today,

